{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbb3xBdhsL9W8zJP33ns", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Banker's rounding** (round-half-to-even, IEEE 754's default) rounds to the nearest value as usual. When a number falls exactly halfway, it rounds to the nearest **even** digit.\n\n| Value | Round half up | Banker's |\n|-------|---------------|----------|\n| 2.5 \u2192 integer | 3 | **2** |\n| 3.5 \u2192 integer | 4 | **4** |\n| 0.125 \u2192 2 dp | 0.13 | **0.12** |\n| 0.135 \u2192 2 dp | 0.14 | **0.14** |\n\n**Why accountants like it**\n\n- **No systematic bias.** With round-half-up, every tie goes up. Over thousands of line items, the totals drift upward. Under banker's rounding, ties go up about half the time and down about half the time, so the errors roughly cancel.\n- **Better aggregates.** Sums, averages and tax totals over large datasets stay closer to the true unrounded figures.\n- **It's a standard.** IEEE 754, many tax and financial regulations, and the default modes in many libraries use it. Examples are Python's `round()` and `decimal.ROUND_HALF_EVEN`, and .NET's `Math.Round`. Using the same mode makes results reproducible and auditable.\n\n**Caveats**\n\n- It only matters for exact ties. For most values it behaves like ordinary rounding.\n- Binary floats can't represent most decimals exactly, so use a decimal type (`Decimal`, `BigDecimal`, integer cents) for money. Otherwise 0.125 may work as a tie but 0.135 won't.\n- Some contexts, such as certain retail or jurisdiction-specific rules, mandate half-up. Check which mode the rules require.\n\nThis project's memory also records that tax calculations use `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`. I haven't opened that memory, so I can't speak to its details."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7376, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_